# A path write whose right side is a call with an effect, in native L2: the receiving place is proven at translation,
# the call is not run by the preparation, and it runs once at the statement (the base `shared` is read before the right
# side; the body of bump is the statement's own right side, not a second evaluation). bump adds 1 to Counter\n and
# returns n + 14: once gives n 1 and seen 15; a second evaluation (a replay after a failed route, or an evaluation by
# the preparation) would give n 2 and seen 16. interp, native and forced answer the same (the book,
# #frame-and-graph-assignment; #binding-caller-place: the name is a place of the caller, not a re-evaluation).
(
    Counter:
        int: n 0
    Observed:
        int: seen 0
)
fn: bump () int
    @: shared node[0]
    shared\Counter\n := shared\Counter\n + 1
    return: shared\Counter\n + 14
sub: main ()
    @: shared node[0]
    shared\Observed\seen := bump()
main()
